
=== Does the white-balance lock reach the die, and does it hold? (#30, #33) ===

clock     : 320 MHz sys
sensor id : 0x82

-- bring-up --
  sensor id : 0x82  (legacy table)
  firmware  : 2023-03-03, fpga rev 32

-- warm-up, auto loops running --
  settled at luma 131

-- the handle, before anything is asked of the die --
  ladder at rest
    up      5   4   7  19  43  86
    down    5   5   7  20  44  87
    wobble 0, retrace 1 | H1 NO  H2 NO  -> NO HANDLE
  -- recovery ladder --
     rung 0: apply the auto mask a second time, no reset
  ladder after rung 0
    up     19  37  71 118 123 115
    down   21  39  73 119 129 115
    wobble 1, retrace 6 | H1 NO  H2 NO  -> NO HANDLE
     rung 1: 0x07 bit 1, the documented I2C reset
  ladder after rung 1
    up     21  41  75 122 176 222
    down   21  41  76 123 177 222
    wobble 1, retrace 1 | H1 yes  H2 yes  -> HANDLE
  recovered on rung 1. The run below stands on a board that needed one,
  which is worth knowing when reading it.

-- stage A: sweep 1024 addresses from 0x3000, WB free against WB locked --
  visit 0 done  (WB free)
  visit 1 done  (WB LOCKED)
  visit 2 done  (WB LOCKED)
  visit 3 done  (WB free)
  visit 4 done  (WB free)
  visit 5 done  (WB LOCKED)

  W0 positive control: 0x300a and 0x300b read 0x36 and 0x4c in all six visits: yes

  sensor   WB free        WB locked      verdict
  0x301b   0e 0e 0e      0d 0d 0d      W1 and W2 - RESPONDS
  0x332b   10 10 10      18 18 18      W1 and W2 - RESPONDS

  2 responded to the WB bit, 0 separated without being still, 2 wobbled without
  separating, 1020 never moved.

-- the frame in each arm, three channels --
  WB free     R  39  G  46  B  39   spread 7
  WB locked   R  37  G  49  B  25   spread 24
  20260825-camlock saw 130 127 129 go to 100 158 122 on this operation.
  ladder after stage A
    up      4   7  16  37  73 123
    down    4   7  16  37  73 123
    wobble 1, retrace 0 | H1 yes  H2 yes  -> HANDLE

-- stage D: 40 rounds an arm on the 2 registers stage A named --
  repeat wobble, three back-to-back reads, worst over the 2: 0
  block 0, WB LOCKED done
  block 1, WB free   done
  block 2, WB LOCKED done

  sensor   locked  free  locked   (excursion over 40 rounds each)
  0x301b      0      0      1
  0x332b      0      0      0
  ladder after stage D
    up      5   8  17  38  76 126
    down    5   8  17  39  77 126
    wobble 1, retrace 1 | H1 yes  H2 yes  -> HANDLE

=== what this run says ===

  THE HANDLE NEEDED RUNG 1. i2crec's claim was rung 2 alone; this board does not.
  W1+W2  2 registers in 0x3000-0x33ff respond to the WB bit.
  W3     locked arms still within the repeat wobble: NO
         free arm moved beyond it:                    no

  THE LOCK DOES NOT HOLD. Registers the WB bit moves keep moving with it
  off, by more than a back-to-back read wobbles. Turning the AWB off is
  therefore NOT sufficient for #30, and the walk it was supposed to remove
  has no remaining suspect on the list this repo has been working down.
  That is worth more than a confirmation would have been. Reproduce it
  before acting on it.

  NOT CLAIMED: what the responding registers ARE. This probe names addresses
  that follow a bit, which is not the same as reading a datasheet. 0x3002 and
  0x3003 are exposure and were identified by writing a value and reading it
  back; nothing here does that for white balance, because there is no write
  path to the die.
